#AUTO 36
#NEEDED 0
# RK817 LDO voltage read + set-path test (issue 114, layer 3).
#
# This is the "active LDO programming" capability: setting a power rail
# to a precise voltage (what you'd use for I/O levels, DVFS, etc.).
# Unlike PWM — which dims by switching on/off fast (varying TIME) — an
# LDO holds a steady, exact VOLTAGE.
#
# The probe reads and decodes all nine of the RK817's LDO output
# voltages (a snapshot of the board's power rails: look for sensible
# values like 1800, 3300), then exercises the new set abstraction
# NON-DESTRUCTIVELY — it programs LDO1 back to its own current value, so
# the encode/write/read-back path is proven without moving any rail.
# A real voltage change is a deliberate step for a rail known to be safe.
#
# The logic is rk817_ldo_get_mv / rk817_ldo_set_mv, exercised by the
# pmic_ldo_test CALL target in src/019-probe-engine.c. Watch for the
# voltage list and "SET PATH OK".

CALL pmic_ldo_test
